//
//  Program:    govax, a "Virtual VAX" written in Go
//
//  Module:     DEBUG.HELP
//
//  Purpose:    The documentation for the govax debugger, read by the
//              debugger's HELP command (DBG> HELP). The format is the
//              same as VAX.HELP's, whose preamble explains it: a "$" line
//              holds a topic's key, one four-character word for each
//              word typed after HELP.
//
//  History:    10/05/26    Started, with the commands the debugger has so
//                          far: EXIT, QUIT, HELP, and @ (docs/PHASE-42.md,
//                          subtask 3).
//              10/05/26    Added GO, CALL, and STEP (subtask 4).
//              10/05/26    STEP as VMS's (subtask 7).
//              10/05/26    Every command and qualifier the debugger has
//                          (subtask 15).
//

$HELP
  The govax debugger examines and controls the emulated VAX. It is modeled
  on the VMS debugger, and its commands follow that debugger's syntax. Type
  HELP topic for more about a command, and HELP topic keyword for a
  keyword or a qualifier of it (HELP SET BREAK, HELP EXAMINE /INSTRUCTION).

  Additional information available:

  @           CALL        CANCEL      CLEAR       DEPOSIT     EVALUATE
  EXAMINE     EXIT        GO          HELP        QUIT        SET
  SHOW        STEP        SYMBOLIZE

  Other topics:

  EXPRESSIONS     How an address or a value is written
  REGISTERS       The names of the registers
  RADIX           The numbers' radix
  BREAKPOINTS     The kinds of eventpoints and what they report

  The debugger is a separate command set from the console's: the VMS
  command line (DIRECTORY, MACRO, LINK, RUN, ...) isn't available here.
  EXIT returns to the console, where it is.

$EXPR
$EXPRESSIONS
  An address or a value is an expression, which may hold:

    numbers          in the input radix (hexadecimal unless SET RADIX says
                     otherwise); ^X, ^D, ^O, and ^B force a radix, as in
                     MACRO (^D35)
    symbols          a routine, label, or data name from the program's
                     debug symbols, or the console's symbol table
    path names       MODULE\ROUTINE\LABEL, as in DBGDIS\START\LOOP
    lines            %LINE n, or MODULE\%LINE n, the code of a source line
    registers        R0 to R11, AP, FP, SP, PC, PSL (also %R0 and so on),
                     as their values; PC alone is the program counter
    .                the current location: the address EXAMINE, DEPOSIT, or
                     SET BASE last used
    .name            the contents at an address: .PC, .R1+4
    operators        + - * / (and parentheses)

  An address range is start:end (EXAMINE A:B), and a list is separated by
  commas (EXAMINE A,B). A routine's name stands for the address after its
  entry mask when a breakpoint is set on it.

$REGI
$REGISTERS
  The registers the debugger knows:

    R0 to R11, AP (R12), FP (R13), SP (R14), PC (R15)   general registers
    PSL                                                 processor status

  and the privileged registers: P0BR P0LR P1BR P1LR SBR SLR PCBB SCBB IPL
  ASTLVL SIRR SISR ICCS NICR ICR TODR RXCS RXDB TXCS TXDB TBDR SAVISP SAVPC
  SAVPSL WCSA WCSB MAPEN TBIA TBIS PMR SID TBCHK.

  EXAMINE, DEPOSIT, and SHOW show or change them, by name.

$RADI
$RADIX
  The debugger reads numbers, and shows them, in a radix: hexadecimal until
  SET RADIX says otherwise. The input radix is the one numbers typed are
  read in; the output radix is the one values are shown in. SET RADIX sets
  both (or SET RADIX/INPUT, SET RADIX/OUTPUT, one of them), SHOW RADIX
  shows them, and CANCEL RADIX restores hexadecimal. The console has a
  radix of its own (SET RADIX there); the two don't affect each other.

$BREA
$BREAKPOINTS
  The debugger has three kinds of eventpoint:

    breakpoints    stop the program before an instruction runs
                   (SET BREAK), or when a class of instruction is reached
                   (/CALL, /BRANCH, /RETURN, /LINE, /INSTRUCTION), or when
                   an exception is signaled (/EXCEPTION), or, govax's own,
                   when a machine fault is about to be delivered (/FAULT)
    tracepoints    report where the program is and go on (SET TRACE)
    watchpoints    stop after the instruction that changes a location
                   (SET WATCH)

  The report says where, in the names the program's debug symbols give:
  "break at DBGDIS\START\%LINE 42" or "trace on lines at ...". SHOW BREAK,
  SHOW TRACE, and SHOW WATCH list them, and CANCEL takes them away.

$@
$INCL
  Runs the debugger commands in a command file.

  Format:

    @file-spec

  The file is read a line at a time, as typed at the DBG> prompt. If a
  command in it ends the debugger session (EXIT), the lines after it are
  console commands.

$CALL
  Calls a routine under the debugger.

  Format:

    CALL[/STEP] routine [(argument[,argument...])]

  The arguments are pushed as a CALLS instruction pushes them. The routine
  runs until it returns, a breakpoint stops it, or Ctrl-C interrupts it.

  /STEP runs only the routine's first instruction and stops. (/BREAK,
  /DEBUG, and /DBG mean the same.)

$CANC
$CLEA
  Removes eventpoints and settings: the debugger's CANCEL. CLEAR is a
  synonym for it.

  Format:

    CANCEL keyword [qualifiers] [parameter]

  Additional information available:

  BREAK  INTERRUPT  MEMORY  MODE  RADIX  SOURCE  TB  TRACE  WATCH

$CANC,BREA
$CLEA,BREA
  Removes breakpoints.

  Format:

    CANCEL BREAK [qualifier] [address[,address...]]

  With addresses, removes the breakpoints there. With a qualifier, removes
  the breakpoints of that kind:

    /ALL            every breakpoint
    /CALL           the breakpoints on calls
    /BRANCH         the breakpoints on branches
    /LINE           the breakpoints on source lines
    /RETURN         the breakpoints on returns
    /EXCEPTION      the breakpoint on exceptions
    /INSTRUCTION[=(opcode,...)]
                    the instruction breakpoints (of the opcodes named, or
                    all of them)
    /FAULT[=code]   govax's fault breakpoints (the one for the fault code,
                    or all of them)

  Cancelling when none is set is %DEBUG-I-NOBREAKS.

$CANC,TRAC
$CLEA,TRAC
  Removes tracepoints.

  Format:

    CANCEL TRACE [qualifier] [address[,address...]]

  The qualifiers are /ALL, /CALL, /BRANCH, /LINE, /RETURN, and
  /INSTRUCTION[=(opcode,...)], as CANCEL BREAK has them.

$CANC,WATC
$CLEA,WATC
  Removes watchpoints.

  Format:

    CANCEL WATCH [/ALL] [location[,location...]]

$CANC,SOUR
$CLEA,SOUR
  Forgets the directories SET SOURCE named, so source files are looked for
  where the image says they are.

  Format:

    CANCEL SOURCE

$CANC,RADI
$CLEA,RADI
  Restores the radix to hexadecimal, for input and output.

  Format:

    CANCEL RADIX

$CANC,MODE
$CLEA,MODE
  Restores SET MODE's defaults (symbolic names, operands explained, and the
  rest).

  Format:

    CANCEL MODE

$CANC,INTE
$CLEA,INTE
  Removes a queued interrupt (govax's own).

  Format:

    CANCEL INTERRUPT [/ALL] [vector]

  vector is the interrupt's SCB offset, in hexadecimal. /ALL removes every
  queued interrupt.

$CANC,TB
$CLEA,TB
  Empties the translation buffer (govax's own), the cache of recent virtual
  to physical address translations. The next access to each page walks the
  page tables again.

  Format:

    CANCEL TB

$CANC,MEMO
$CLEA,MEMO
  CANCEL MEMORY/STATISTICS zeroes the memory system's counters (govax's
  own). (At the console, CLEAR MEMORY with no qualifier is a different
  command, which zeroes the memory itself.)

  Format:

    CANCEL MEMORY/STATISTICS

$DEPO
$D
  Stores a value at a location: a register, or an address.

  Format:

    DEPOSIT[/type] location = value
    DEPOSIT/ASCII[:count] location = "string"

  The value is in the input radix. The type is /BYTE, /WORD, /LONGWORD, or
  /QUADWORD; without one it is the type of the data the location names in
  the program's debug symbols, else a longword. /ASCII stores a quoted
  string, of the count given (padded with blanks) or its own length.

  Examples:

    DEPOSIT R0 = 5
    DEPOSIT PC = 200
    DEPOSIT/BYTE 2000 = 0FF
    DEPOSIT COUNT = 10

$EVAL
  Shows the value of an expression.

  Format:

    EVALUATE[/radix] expression
    EVALUATE/ADDRESS expression

  The value is shown in the output radix, or in /HEXADECIMAL, /DECIMAL,
  /OCTAL, or /BINARY. With a data name, the value is its contents.
  /ADDRESS shows the address instead.

$EXAM
$EX
  Shows what is at a location.

  Format:

    EXAMINE[/qualifier...] [location[,location...]]
    EXAMINE[/qualifier...] start:end

  A location is an expression (HELP EXPRESSIONS): an address, a name, a
  register. A range (start:end) shows each item from start through end. With
  no location, EXAMINE shows the next location after the last one shown.

  Without /INSTRUCTION, EXAMINE shows data: typed by the program's debug
  symbols where the location names a datum (arrays as TABLE[0:3], strings
  through their descriptors), and otherwise by the type qualifier given, in
  the output radix.

  Additional information available:

  /INSTRUCTION  /OPERANDS  /BYTE  /WORD  /LONGWORD  /QUADWORD  /ASCII
  /HEXADECIMAL  /DECIMAL  /OCTAL  /BINARY  /PSL  /PTE  /CONSTANTS
  /SHAREABLE  /SYMBOLIC

$EXAM,/INS
  Shows the machine instruction at each location, in the VMS debugger's
  layout, with names from the program's debug symbols:

    DBGDIS\START\%LINE 42:  MOVL     S^#0A,R2

  A range lists every instruction in it.

$EXAM,/OPE
  With /INSTRUCTION, explains each operand: its value, and where it is.
  /OPERANDS=FULL says more. /NOOPERANDS (the default, unless SET MODE
  OPERANDS) leaves them out.

$EXAM,/BYT
$EXAM,/WOR
$EXAM,/LON
$EXAM,/QUA
  Show each item as a byte, a word (two bytes), a longword (four), or a
  quadword (eight), when the program's debug symbols don't give the type.

$EXAM,/ASC
  Shows bytes as ASCII text. /ASCII:count shows count bytes.

$EXAM,/HEX
$EXAM,/DEC
$EXAM,/OCT
$EXAM,/BIN
  Show the value in hexadecimal, decimal, octal, or binary, for this
  command, instead of the output radix.

$EXAM,/PSL
  govax's own: shows a longword as a processor status longword, field by
  field.

$EXAM,/PTE
  govax's own: shows a longword as a page table entry: its valid bit,
  protection, modify bit, owner, and page frame number.

$EXAM,/CON
  govax's own, with /INSTRUCTION: names a constant by the symbol that
  defines it (MOVL S^#DBGDIS\LIMIT,R2), which the VMS debugger doesn't.

$EXAM,/SHA
  govax's own, with /INSTRUCTION: names a G^ reference's routine in a
  shareable image (CALLS S^#01,G^LIB$PUT_OUTPUT).

$EXAM,/SYM
  Accepted, as VMS has it. SET MODE SYMBOLIC or NOSYMBOLIC chooses whether
  an instruction's addresses are names.

$EXIT
  Ends the debugger session and returns to the console. The machine is
  left as it is.

  Format:

    EXIT

$GO
  Runs the program, from the current PC or from an address.

  Format:

    GO [address]

  The program runs until it halts, a breakpoint stops it, or Ctrl-C
  interrupts it. A breakpoint at the address the run starts from doesn't
  stop it at once.

$HELP,HELP
  Displays help on a debugger command.

  Format:

    HELP [topic ...]

  HELP alone lists the topics.

$QUIT
  Ends the debugger session and returns to the console, as EXIT does.
  (On VMS, QUIT skips the program's exit handlers, which EXIT runs.
  govax's debugger doesn't run exit handlers yet, so the two are the
  same.)

  Format:

    QUIT

$SET
$SE
  Changes what the debugger does or shows.

  Format:

    SET keyword [qualifiers] [parameters]

  Additional information available:

  BASE     BREAK    FAULT    MODE     MODULE   PSL      PTE      RADIX
  SOURCE   STEP     TRACE    VM       WATCH

  (SET BREAKPOINT, SET TRACEPOINT, and SET WATCHPOINT are the same as
  SET BREAK, SET TRACE, and SET WATCH.)

$SET,BREA
$SE,BREA
  Sets breakpoints: the program stops before the instruction runs.

  Format:

    SET BREAK [qualifiers] [address[,address...]] [WHEN (condition)]
              [DO (command[;command...])]

  An address may be a routine's name: the break is after its entry mask, at
  its first instruction. A qualifier picks a class instead of an address;
  one at most is given:

    /CALL           each call instruction (CALLS, JSB, ...)
    /BRANCH         each branch instruction
    /RETURN routine each RET that ends a call of the routine named
    /LINE           each source line's first instruction
    /INSTRUCTION[=(opcode,...)]
                    each instruction (of the opcodes named, if any)
    /EXCEPTION      each exception signaled
    /FAULT=code     govax's own: when the machine fault with that code
                    (hexadecimal) is about to be delivered

  /AFTER:n skips the first n times the breakpoint is reached. /TEMPORARY
  removes it when it first stops the program. WHEN (condition) stops only
  when the condition is nonzero; DO runs the commands at the stop.

  The report is "break at location", "break on lines at ...", and so on.
  A break at the address a run starts from doesn't stop it at once.

  Examples:

    SET BREAK FACT
    SET BREAK/TEMPORARY DBGDIS\START\%LINE 47
    SET BREAK/INSTRUCTION=(MOVC3)
    SET BREAK SUB2 WHEN (R0 EQL 5) DO (EXAMINE R0)

$SET,TRAC
$SE,TRAC
  Sets tracepoints: the program reports where it is and goes on.

  Format:

    SET TRACE [qualifiers] [address[,address...]] [WHEN (condition)]
              [DO (command[;command...])]

  The qualifiers are /AFTER:n, /TEMPORARY, /CALL, /BRANCH, /LINE, /RETURN,
  and /INSTRUCTION[=(opcode,...)], as SET BREAK has them. SET TRACE
  /INSTRUCTION traces every instruction. The report is "trace at
  location", "trace on lines at ...", and so on.

$SET,WATC
$SE,WATC
  Sets watchpoints: the program stops after an instruction that changes a
  location.

  Format:

    SET WATCH [/AFTER:n] [/TEMPORARY] location [WHEN (condition)]
              [DO (command[;command...])]

  The location's size comes from the program's debug symbols: a scalar by
  its type, an array (BUFFER[0:15]) item by item, anything else a
  longword. The report gives the old and new values:

    watch of DBGCMD\COUNT at DBGCMD\START\%LINE 42
       old value: 00000000
       new value: 00000001

$SET,STEP
$SE,STEP
  Sets what STEP does when it isn't told.

  Format:

    SET STEP keyword[,keyword...]

  The keywords are:

    LINE | INSTRUCTION     how far a step goes
    OVER | INTO (IN) | RETURN
                           what to do at a call (RETURN is govax's: run to
                           the return of the routine)
    SILENT | NOSILENT      whether the stop is reported
    SOURCE | NOSOURCE      whether the source line is shown

  SHOW STEP shows the settings.

$SET,SOUR
$SE,SOUR
  Names the directories searched for a program's source files, when they
  aren't where the image says.

  Format:

    SET SOURCE directory[,directory...]

  SHOW SOURCE lists them, and CANCEL SOURCE forgets them.

$SET,MODE
$SE,MODE
  Sets display modes, and govax's access mode.

  Format:

    SET MODE keyword[,keyword...]

  Display modes (the VMS debugger's):

    SYMBOLIC | NOSYMBOLIC  whether an address is shown as a name
    OPERANDS[=FULL|BRIEF] | NOOPERANDS
                           whether EXAMINE/INSTRUCTION explains operands
    LINE | NOLINE          whether the debugger works by source line
    G_FLOAT | NOG_FLOAT    the floating format
    SCROLL, DYNAMIC, INTERRUPT, and their NO forms are accepted; screen
    mode isn't available.

  Access modes (govax's own): KERNEL, EXECUTIVE, SUPERVISOR, USER switch
  the CPU's mode and its stack pointer, and ISP the interrupt stack.

  SHOW MODE shows the settings, and CANCEL MODE restores the defaults.

$SET,RADI
$SE,RADI
  Sets the radix for numbers typed and shown.

  Format:

    SET RADIX [/INPUT|/OUTPUT] radix

  radix is DECIMAL, HEXADECIMAL, OCTAL, or BINARY (also 10, 16, 8, 2).
  With no qualifier it sets both. HELP RADIX says more.

$SET,MODU
$SE,MODU
  Notes that a module's symbols are wanted.

  Format:

    SET MODULE module[,module...]
    SET MODULE/ALL

  govax reads every module's symbols when it loads an image, so this
  changes only the "set" column of SHOW MODULE and which modules a SHOW
  SYMBOL with no IN looks in.

$SET,PSL
$SE,PSL
  Changes fields of the processor status longword (govax's own).

  Format:

    SET PSL field=value[,field=value...]

  The fields are C, V, Z, N, T, IV, FU, DV, IPL, and MOD (also CUR_MOD).
  Values are in the input radix.

  Example:

    SET PSL IPL=1F, N=1

$SET,PTE
$SET,PAGE
$SE,PTE
$SE,PAGE
  Changes page table entries (govax's own).

  Format:

    SET PTE address [TO address] field=value[,field=value...]

  The fields are VALID (V), PROT, MODIFY (M), OWNER (OWN), SOFT (S), and PFN
  (PAGE), the page frame number. PROT is a number or the name SHOW PTE prints (PTE$K_KW). With
  TO, every 512-byte page from the first address through the second is
  changed.

  Example:

    SET PTE 200 TO 400 VALID=1,PFN=10

$SET,FAUL
$SET,HIST
$SE,FAUL
$SE,HIST
  Sets how many machine faults are remembered for SHOW EXCEPTIONS (govax's
  own).

  Format:

    SET FAULT count

$SET,VM
$SET,MAPE
$SE,VM
$SE,MAPE
  SET VM turns address translation (memory mapping) on, and SET NOVM turns
  it off (govax's own). The privileged register MAPEN is the switch.

  Format:

    SET VM
    SET NOVM

$SET,BASE
$SE,BASE
  Sets the current location, where EXAMINE with no location starts (govax's
  own).

  Format:

    SET BASE address

$SHOW
$SH
  Displays what the debugger knows.

  Format:

    SHOW keyword [qualifiers] [parameters]

  Additional information available:

  BASE       BREAK      CALLS      CLOCK      CPU_STATUS EXCEPTIONS
  IMAGE      LANGUAGE   MAPS       MEMORY     MODE       MODULE
  PAGE       PSL        RADIX      REGIONS    REGISTERS  SCB
  SCOPE      SHIM       SOURCE     STACK      STEP       SYMBOL
  TB         TRACE      WATCH

  and the register names: SHOW R0, SHOW PC, SHOW P0BR, and so on. SHOW SP,
  KSP, ESP, SSP, USP, and ISP dump the longwords on a stack.

$SHOW,BREA
$SH,BREA
  Lists the breakpoints, with their counts and clauses.

  Format:

    SHOW BREAK

$SHOW,TRAC
$SH,TRAC
  Lists the tracepoints.

  Format:

    SHOW TRACE

$SHOW,WATC
$SH,WATC
  Lists the watchpoints.

  Format:

    SHOW WATCH

$SHOW,STEP
$SH,STEP
  Shows what STEP does when it isn't told (SET STEP).

  Format:

    SHOW STEP

$SHOW,SOUR
$SH,SOUR
  Lists the directories SET SOURCE named.

  Format:

    SHOW SOURCE

$SHOW,CALL
$SH,CALL
  Lists the call frames, innermost first, one row each: module, routine,
  line, and the PC relative to the routine and absolute. A call frame is the
  record CALLS and CALLG leave on the stack, which links each routine to its
  caller.

  Format:

    SHOW CALLS [count]

  With a count, only that many frames. In a JSB subroutine, which has no
  frame of its own, the row is the routine that holds it.

$SHOW,STAC
$SH,STAC
  Describes each call frame in full: the saved registers, argument count, and
  condition handler (VMS's SHOW STACK).

  Format:

    SHOW STACK [count]

  govax's longword dump of a stack is SHOW SP, KSP, ESP, SSP, USP, or ISP.

$SHOW,SP
$SHOW,KSP
$SHOW,ESP
$SHOW,SSP
$SHOW,USP
$SHOW,ISP
$SH,SP
$SH,KSP
$SH,ESP
$SH,SSP
$SH,USP
$SH,ISP
  Dumps the longwords on a stack (govax's own): SHOW SP the current one, and
  the others the stack of the mode named (kernel, executive, supervisor,
  user, and the interrupt stack).

  Format:

    SHOW KSP [count] [/ALL]

  count is how many longwords, in hexadecimal; /ALL shows the whole stack.

$SHOW,REGI
$SHOW,REG
$SH,REGI
$SH,REG
  SHOW REGISTERS shows the general registers and the PSL (govax's own).
  A single register, general or privileged, is SHOW name (SHOW R3, SHOW PC,
  SHOW P0BR). HELP REGISTERS lists the names.

  SHOW REGIONS shows the limits of the P0, P1, and S0 regions image
  activation uses (govax's own).

  Format:

    SHOW REGISTERS
    SHOW REGIONS

$SHOW,PSL
$SH,PSL
  Shows the processor status longword as a number and as its fields
  (govax's own).

  Format:

    SHOW PSL

$SHOW,CPU_
$SH,CPU_
  Shows the registers and the PSL, the stack pointer of each mode, and the
  privileged registers, then the current location (govax's own).

  Format:

    SHOW CPU_STATUS

$SHOW,CLOC
$SH,CLOC
  Shows the interval clock and the time-of-day register (govax's own).

  Format:

    SHOW CLOCK

$SHOW,BASE
$SH,BASE
  Shows the current location, where EXAMINE with no location starts
  (govax's own).

  Format:

    SHOW BASE

$SHOW,MEMO
$SHOW,VM
$SH,MEMO
$SH,VM
  Shows the size of physical memory and, once VMINIT has made page tables,
  how much of each region (P0, P1, S0) is used (govax's own). With
  /STATISTICS, counters of the memory system's work; /RUNTIME and /FULL
  show more.

  Format:

    SHOW MEMORY [/STATISTICS] [/RUNTIME] [/FULL]

$SHOW,MAPS
$SH,MAPS
  Reports that govax has no field-offset map of RMS's data structures to
  show (the VMS command of this name shows one; govax's RMS code keeps no
  such table).

  Format:

    SHOW MAPS

$SHOW,TB
$SH,TB
  Shows the translation buffer: its hit and miss counts, and each entry in
  use (govax's own). The translation buffer is the cache of recent virtual
  to physical address translations. Flushes counts the times the whole
  buffer was emptied (MTPR to TBIA); PFlushes the times only its process
  (P0 and P1) translations were, as LDPCTX does when it switches processes.

  Format:

    SHOW TB

$SHOW,PAGE
$SHOW,PTE
$SH,PAGE
$SH,PTE
  Shows the page table entry for an address (govax's own).

  Format:

    SHOW PAGE [/READ|/WRITE] address

  The entry's fields are shown, with its protection by name (PTE$K_KW).
  /READ and /WRITE say which access the entry is checked for.

$SHOW,SCB
$SH,SCB
  Shows the system control block (SCB), which holds the address of each
  exception and interrupt routine (govax's own).

  Format:

    SHOW SCB [/ALL]

  Only the vectors in use, unless /ALL.

$SHOW,SHIM
$SH,SHIM
  Shows the shims: the stubs that stand in for shareable images' routines,
  and what each reaches, native code or a microkernel routine (govax's
  own).

  Format:

    SHOW SHIM

$SHOW,EXCE
$SHOW,FAUL
$SH,EXCE
$SH,FAUL
  Shows the machine faults and exceptions recently taken, and the interrupts
  pending (govax's own). SET FAULT chooses how many faults are kept.

  Format:

    SHOW EXCEPTIONS

$SHOW,MODE
$SH,MODE
  Shows the display modes (SET MODE), and, on a last line, govax's access
  mode.

  Format:

    SHOW MODE

$SHOW,RADI
$SH,RADI
  Shows the input and output radix.

  Format:

    SHOW RADIX

$SHOW,IMAG
$SH,IMAG
  Lists the loaded images, the main one marked with an asterisk: whether
  it has debug symbols (set), and its first and last address.

  Format:

    SHOW IMAGE [/FULL]

  /FULL (govax's own) adds each image's counts of modules and routines.

$SHOW,MODU
$SH,MODU
  Lists the modules of the program's debug symbols, whether each is set,
  and the size of its symbol records.

  Format:

    SHOW MODULE

$SHOW,SYMB
$SH,SYMB
  Lists the symbols matching a pattern.

  Format:

    SHOW SYMBOL [/ADDRESS] [/TYPE] pattern [IN module[,module...]]

  The pattern may have * and % wildcards, and is matched with the symbol's
  name, or its whole path name if it has a backslash. The symbols are the
  routines first, then the data and labels, then the program sections, each
  by name. /ADDRESS shows each one's address, and /TYPE its data type.

  A pattern that matches no symbol of the program is tried against the
  console's symbol table, and then is %DEBUG-E-NOSYMBOL.

$SHOW,SCOP
$SH,SCOP
  Lists the call levels names are looked up in, innermost (level 0, marked
  with an asterisk) first. A routine entered more than once is numbered
  (FACT 1).

  Format:

    SHOW SCOPE

$SHOW,LANG
$SH,LANG
  Shows the language of the current module (MACRO, for the programs govax
  runs).

  Format:

    SHOW LANGUAGE

$STEP
$ST
$S
  Executes one or more steps of the program and stops again. A step is
  one source line, or one instruction, as SET STEP says.

  Format:

    STEP[/qualifier...] [count]

  count is the number of steps to take (one if none is given); only where
  the last one ends is reported.

  /LINE (the default) steps to the first instruction of the next source
  line. /INSTRUCTION steps one instruction. Where the program has no line
  numbers, a step is one instruction either way.

  /OVER (the default) runs a call (CALLS, CALLG, JSB, BSBB, BSBW, or a
  change-mode instruction) to its end, and the step stops after it. /INTO
  (also /IN) follows the call into the routine.

  /RETURN runs until the RET that ends the routine's call frame is about
  to execute. The wait belongs to that frame: a deeper call's RET doesn't
  end it, and it stays in force across breakpoints and other STEPs until
  the RET is reached.

  /BRANCH and /CALL run until the next branch or call instruction is about
  to execute.

  /SILENT reports nothing. /NOSILENT (the default) reports where the
  step stopped. /[NO]SOURCE chooses whether the source line is shown with
  it.

  With a qualifier left out, STEP uses what SET STEP chose.

$SYMB
  Shows each name the debugger has for an address.

  Format:

    SYMBOLIZE address

  The program's routines, labels, and lines come first, then the names in
  the console's symbol table. An address with no name is shown as itself.

  Example:

    SYMBOLIZE 5A0
